      "slug": "829ce596dfecf2d9",
      "title": "BMW Executive in 2023: No Plans to Sell in-Vehicle Ads",
      "titleZh": "BMW Executive in 2023: No Plans to Sell in-Vehicle Ads",
      "link": "https://www.mediapost.com/publications/article/391876/bmw-exec-no-plans-to-sell-in-vehicle-ads.html",
      "sourceName": "daringfireball.net",
      "sourceUrl": "https://daringfireball.net",
      "pubDate": "2026-08-06T16:46:02.000Z",
      "category": "other",
      "keywords": [],
      "score": {
        "relevance": 5,
        "quality": 5,
        "timeliness": 5,
        "total": 15
      },
      "summary": "BMW Executive in 2023: No Plans to Sell in-Vehicle Ads",
      "summaryEn": "",
      "reason": "",
      "fullTextStatus": "ok",
      "fullText": {
        "byline": "Tanya Gazdik",
        "partial": false,
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "li",
            "text": "by \n                                        , \n                                    \n                                \n                                December 13, 2023"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Despite the ever-expanding screens\nin its vehicles, including an available 31-inch backseat theatre screen, BMW has no plans to sell advertising on any of them."
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Radio commercials are intrusive enough, says Stephan\nDurach, the brand's senior vice president, connected company development."
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "“At the end of the day, I think your car is your last private space,” Durach told a small group\nof media members last week during a roundtable discussion. “It’s where you can do whatever you want by yourself -- you have the right temperature, the music you want. To say I'm selling\nthe screen to play a commercial -- I don't see it. It’s a private space.”"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "BMW has not been afraid to push the envelope to find new ways to make money. The automaker was\nthe first to charge a fee for heated seats in its vehicles in some overseas markets."
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "advertisement"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "advertisement"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "But earlier this fall, the automaker dropped the heated seats subscription option after\ncustomers expressed discontent for having to pay to unlock existing functions in their cars."
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Other potential moneymakers for the German automaker could be a subscription\nservice tied to offering a location-based scent experience in cars, or the ability to link augmented reality headsets to vehicles in motion for backseat passengers. Both technologies were shown to the\nmedia last week."
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "The scent experience would link which scent the occupants experience to GPS data. For example, when driving by the ocean, the car would supply a beach-inspired scent. Or if\nthe driver has entered the address for a gym into the navigation system, the vehicle could offer scents that would energize the driver."
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "The automaker also highlighted technology already in\nproduction. BMW has already developed ConnectedRide Smartglasses which give motorcycle riders a head-up display inside of their helmet with information like navigation, speed and gear — similar\nto what is displayed on a head-up display in a vehicle."
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Chock full of technology and making its North American debut was the BMW Vision Neue Klasse concept car, which will go\ninto production in 2025."
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "It includes a minimalistic interior devoid of knobs and switches. Instead, information is communicated via a panoramic head-up display screen that runs the\nentire length of the inside of the windshield and can be seen by passengers. Traditionally, head-up displays have only been in the driver’s visual field."
          }
        ],
        "translations": [
          "by \n                                        , \n                                    \n                                \n                                December 13, 2023",
