        "partial": false,
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "A few months ago, when I was walking around a mall on a ninety-something-degree day. I made a claim to my wife that I thought profound, but she kind of saw through pretty quickly."
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "“If you walked through a mall 20 years ago, everyone would basically look the same,” I said, noting that fashion has barely shifted since the mid-2000s. She called bullshit, noting that folks simply wearing T-shirts and jeans and khakis wasn’t enough to say we still dress the same."
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "But when we caught Nirvanna the Band The Show the Movie recently, I felt some modest vindication. The entire time-travel plot of the movie only really works because of this point I highlighted. If the characters found themselves in 1995, the fashions would be dramatically different; but in 2008, little would change, minus the kinds of phones in their hands."
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "However, it’s clear that something did in fact change since then, and Glen Hansard’s recent passing highlights the lingering background subtlety. Throughout the 2000s, a crop of extremely earnest singer-songwriters found an audience through various means. Dudes like Damien Rice and Damien Jurado come to mind, as do dudes like Josh Rouse and Josh Ritter. (As well as people who don’t share first names.)"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "On the indie-leaning end of things, Iron & Wine, Devendra Banhart, Fleet Foxes, José González, and Bon Iver gave this organic, folky sound a sense of ambition. (Some, like Mark Kozelek, leave us with deep senses of regret.) If you were a white guy with a beard and wanted to express your feelings in song, there were many lanes for your acoustic guitar in the mid-2000s."
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
